cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "AssayPilot: assay-specific experiment planning for protein variant campaigns"
message: >-
  If AssayPilot contributes to published work, please cite it, and cite the
  methods and datasets you used (ESM-2, ProteinGym, FLIP) directly.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jain
    given-names: Anusha
    alias: anushajain2
repository-code: "https://github.com/anushajain2/assay-pilot"
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-10-18"
keywords:
  - protein engineering
  - directed evolution
  - active learning
  - protein language models
  - experiment design
abstract: >-
  AssayPilot plans the next round of a protein engineering campaign from the
  measurements already collected. It validates sequence-measurement inputs
  against a declared reference, chooses an evaluation protocol that matches the
  intended next step, compares a fixed suite of three prediction methods on
  held-out measurements from the same campaign, applies a stated rule that
  prefers the simpler method unless a paired bootstrap interval supports a more
  complex one, and selects a fixed-size batch under explicit constraints. Every
  plan is exported with a run manifest recording inputs, partitions, seeds,
  hyperparameters and predictions, and each round's predictions are scored
  against the measurements that follow before any retraining.
references:
  - type: article
    title: "Benchmarking uncertainty quantification for protein engineering"
    authors:
      - family-names: Greenman
        given-names: Kevin
      - family-names: Amini
        given-names: Ava
      - family-names: Yang
        given-names: Kevin
    journal: "PLOS Computational Biology"
    year: 2025
    doi: "10.1371/journal.pcbi.1012639"
